1. @SpringBootApplication : Main class; combines `@Configuration`, `@EnableAutoConfiguration`, `@ComponentScan`
--> @SpringBootApplication is the entry point of a Spring Boot app that enables auto-configuration,
    component scanning, and Java-based configuration.
Ex: 
@SpringBootApplication
public class UserServiceApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(UserServiceApplication.class, args);
    }
}

|Startup Flow (Simple) :                           |
|--------------------------------------------------
|main() method                                     |
   ↓
|@SpringBootApplication detected                   |
   ↓
|Auto-configuration applied                        |
   ↓
|@ComponentScan runs                               |
   ↓
|Beans created (@Controller, @Service, @Repository)|
   ↓
|Application starts                                |
----------------------------------------------------

2. @Configuration: 
--> @Configuration classes are processed during Spring Boot startup to register beans in the application context,
    before other dependent beans are created.

🔹 Key Points
--> @Configuration classes are processed first to register @Bean methods.
--> Beans inside @Configuration are singleton by default and managed by Spring.
--> @Component beans are created after scanning, so if they depend on a @Bean, it must already exist.
--> @Configuration ensures that even if a @Bean method is called multiple times, same singleton instance is returned (thanks to CGLIB proxy).
Example : 
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public MyService myService() {
        return new MyServiceImpl();
    }
}

@Component
public class MyComponent {

    @Autowired
    private MyService myService; // injected from AppConfig
}
✅ Here:
--> AppConfig (@Configuration) runs first, creates myService bean
--> MyComponent (@Component) is initialized after, and receives the same singleton myService

3. @Bean : 
--> It tells Spring to create and manage an object (bean) returned by a method.

Why @Bean is used : 
--> When you cannot annotate the class with @Component (third-party / library class)
--> When you want full control over object creation
Ex: 
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }
}
✅ Spring creates one instance of PasswordEncoder
✅ Available for @Autowired anywhere

How it works (Simple)
--> Spring internally does: PasswordEncoder encoder = passwordEncoder();
    and stores it in the ApplicationContext.

Q: @Bean vs @Component (Quick): 
-----------------------------------
| `@Bean`                        | `@Component`           |
| ------------------------------ | ---------------------- |
| Method-level.                  | Class-level            |
| Used in `@Configuration` .     | Used directly on class |
| For external / custom creation.| For your own classes   |

4. @Component : 
--> It marks a class as a Spring-managed bean so Spring can automatically create and manage its object.
Ex: 
@Component
public class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail() {
        System.out.println("Email sent");
    }
}
✅ Spring creates the object
✅ You can inject it using @Autowired

Special Types of @Component : 
---------------------------------------------
| Annotation        | Used for             |
| ----------------- | -------------------- |
| `@Service`        | Business logic layer |
| `@Repository`     | DAO / DB layer       |
| `@Controller`     | MVC controller       |
| `@RestController` | REST APIs            |

5. @Autowired : 
--> It tells Spring to automatically inject (provide) the required dependency into a class. or,
--> @Autowired enables dependency injection by automatically injecting required Spring-managed beans.

Q: Why @Autowired is used
--> To achieve Dependency Injection (DI)
--> Removes manual object creation using new
--> Promotes loose coupling

Ex: 
@Service
public class UserService {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;
}

Q: What is Dependency Injection ?
-----------------------------------
 -->  Dependency Injection means instead of a class creating its own objects, Spring provides (injects) them.

🧠 In Very Simple Words

❌ Without DI: UserService service = new UserService();

✅ With DI: Spring creates UserService and injects its dependencies automatically

🔗 What is a Dependency?
--> A dependency is any object a class needs to work.

Ex: UserService depends on UserRepository

❌ Without Dependency Injection (Bad Practice)
public class UserService {
    private UserRepository repo = new UserRepository();
}
❌ Tight coupling
❌ Hard to test
❌ Not flexible

✅ With Dependency Injection (Spring Way)
@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository repo;

    public UserService(UserRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
✔ Loose coupling
✔ Easy testing
✔ Clean architecture

🧩 How Spring Does DI
Spring Container
   ↓
Creates beans
   ↓
Resolves dependencies
   ↓
Injects dependencies

Types of Dependency Injection : 
---------------------------------

1️⃣ Field Injection (Not Recommended)
@Autowired
private UserRepository userRepository;

2️⃣ Constructor Injection ✅ (Best Practice)
@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }
}

3️⃣ Setter Injection
@Autowired
public void setUserRepository(UserRepository userRepository) {
    this.userRepository = userRepository;
}

6. @Value : 
===============
--> @Value is used to inject values from configuration files (application.properties / application.yml) into Spring beans.

🧠 Why @Value is used ? 
--> Read values from config files
--> Avoid hard-coding
--> Externalize environment-specific data

Ex: application.yml
--------------------
app.name: User Service
app.timeout: 30

Java Class 
-----------
@Component
public class AppConfig {

    @Value("${app.name}")
    private String appName;

    @Value("${app.timeout}")
    private int timeout;
}
✅ Spring injects values at runtime

⚠️ Important Notes : 
-----------------------
--> Key must exist, else app fails to start
--> Type conversion is automatic
--> Supports default values

Ex:
@Value("${server.port:8080}")
private int port;

7. @ConfigurationProperties : 
===============================
--> It binds a group of related configuration properties into a strongly-typed Java class.

🧠 Why @ConfigurationProperties is used ?
--> Clean handling of multiple related properties
--> Type-safe configuration
--> Better than many @Value annotations

Example (Industry Standard) :
application.yml
------------------
app:
  name: User Service
  timeout: 30
  max-users: 100

Config Class 
---------------
@Component
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app")
public class AppProperties {

    private String name;
    private int timeout;
    private int maxUsers;

    // getters & setters
}
✅ All app.* values mapped automatically
✅ Type-safe
✅ Clean code

Q: @Value vs @ConfigurationProperties (Interview Table)?

| Feature         | `@Value` | `@ConfigurationProperties`   |
| --------------- | -------- | --------------------------   |
| Multiple values | ❌       | ✅                          |
| Type safety     | ❌       | ✅                          |
| Readability     | ❌       | ✅                          |
| Validation      | ❌       | ✅ (`@Validated`)           |

8. @ComponentScan : 
--> @ComponentScan tells Spring which packages to scan to detect and register Spring beans.

🧠 Why @ComponentScan is used ?
--> To discover beans automatically
--> To define base packages for scanning
--> Required when beans are outside the default package

Ex: 
@ComponentScan(basePackages = "com.ajay.userservice")
public class AppConfig {
}
--> Spring scans : com.ajay.userservice.*

@SpringBootApplication : Default Behavior (VERY IMPORTANT)
➡ Internally includes: @ComponentScan
📌 It scans:
--> The package of the main class
--> All its sub-packages

9. @EnableAutoConfiguration : 
--> It tells Spring Boot to automatically configure beans based on the dependencies present in the classpath.

🧠 Why @EnableAutoConfiguration is used ?
--> Removes manual configuration
--> Automatically sets up:
     1. DataSource
     2. JPA / Hibernate
     3. Spring MVC
     4. Security
     5. Makes Spring Boot plug-and-play

🔄 How it works (Simple) : 

Dependencies (pom.xml)
   ↓
@EnableAutoConfiguration
   ↓
Spring Boot checks classpath
   ↓
Auto-configures required beans

Example:
  1. spring-boot-starter-data-jpa present → JPA configured
  2. MySQL driver present → DataSource configured

❌ Without @EnableAutoConfiguration
--> You must configure everything manually
--> No default beans
--> More boilerplate code

⚠️ Common Interview Traps
1. ❌ Thinking it scans packages → ❌ (That’s @ComponentScan)
2. ❌ Thinking it creates all beans → ❌ (Only relevant ones)

10. @Primary : 
=================
--> It tells Spring which bean should be injected by default when multiple beans of the same type exist.

🧠 Why @Primary is used
--> To resolve ambiguity when more than one bean of the same type is present
--> Avoids NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

Problem Example (Without @Primary) : 
@Component
public class SmsService implements NotificationService {}

@Component
public class EmailService implements NotificationService {}

@Autowired
private NotificationService notificationService;

❌ Spring error: NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

✅ Solution Using @Primary : 
@Component
@Primary
public class EmailService implements NotificationService {}
--> Spring injects EmailService by default

🔁 How it works
  1. Spring sees multiple beans
  2. Picks the one marked with @Primary

⚠️ Important Notes
  1. Works only when multiple beans of same type
  2. Can be overridden using @Qualifier
  3. Applicable on @Component / @Bean

11. @Qualifier : 
--> @Qualifier is used to specify which bean to inject when multiple beans of the same type are available.

🧠 Why @Qualifier is used
--> To remove ambiguity
--> To select a specific implementation
--> Overrides @Primary

🔧 Problem Scenario
 1. interface : 
public interface PaymentService {}

UpiPaymentService: 
--------------------
@Component("upiService")
public class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService {}

CardPaymentService: 
---------------------
@Component("cardService")
public class CardPaymentService implements PaymentService {}

❌ Without @Qualifier
------------------------
@Autowired
private PaymentService paymentService;

❌ Error: NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException

✅ With @Qualifier : 
-----------------------
@Autowired
@Qualifier("upiService")
private PaymentService paymentService;

--> ✔ UpiPaymentService is injected

Q: 🆚 @Qualifier vs @Primary (Interview-Ready) : 

| Feature        | `@Qualifier`                 | `@Primary`              |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Purpose        | Select **specific bean**     | Select **default bean** |
| When used      | At **injection point**       | On **bean definition**  |
| Control        | **Explicit** (local)         | **Implicit** (global)   |
| Multiple beans | Yes                          | Yes (only one primary)  |
| Overrides      | Overrides `@Primary`         | Can be overridden       |
| Best use case  | Different behavior per class | One common default      |
| Scope          | Per injection                | Application-wide        |

12. @PostConstruct : 
--> It runs a method automatically after the bean is created and all dependencies are injected.

🧠 Why @PostConstruct is used
--> To perform initialization logic
--> To validate configuration
--> To load cache / pre-data after startup

🔄 Bean Lifecycle (Simple) : 
Bean created
   ↓
Dependencies injected (@Autowired)
   ↓
@PostConstruct method runs  ✅

Ex: 
@Component
public class CacheLoader {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("Cache loaded");
    }
}
--> Runs once at application startup
--> Runs after DI, not before

⚠️ Important Rules
1. Method must be: I. void.  II. No arguments
2. Only one per bean (recommended)
3. Runs only once

Q: 🆚 @PostConstruct vs Constructor ?

| Feature                | Constructor            | `@PostConstruct` |
| ---------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------- |
| DI completed           | ❌                     | ✅              |
| Access autowired beans | ❌                     | ✅              |
| Called                 | During object creation  | After injection |

13. @PreDestroy : 
--> It runs a method just before a Spring bean is destroyed (when the application is shutting down).

🧠 Why @PreDestroy is used
--> To perform cleanup logic
--> Release resources
--> Close connections gracefully

🔄 Bean Lifecycle (Complete View) : 

Bean created
   ↓
Dependencies injected
   ↓
@PostConstruct
   ↓
Application running
   ↓
@PreDestroy  ✅
   ↓
Bean destroyed

Ex: 
@Component
public class ResourceManager {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("Resources initialized");
    }

    @PreDestroy
    public void cleanup() {
        System.out.println("Resources released");
    }
}
--> ✔ Runs once
--> ✔ Triggered during graceful shutdown

⚠️ Important Rules

Method must be: 1. void  2. No arguments

Executes only if: 1. Context closes normally 2. Not on JVM crash

Q: 🆚 @PreDestroy vs @PostConstruct ?

| Feature | `@PostConstruct`    | `@PreDestroy`           |
| ------- | ------------------- | ----------------------- |
| When    | After bean creation | Before bean destruction |
| Use     | Initialization      | Cleanup                 |

14. @Scope : 
--> @Scope defines how and when Spring creates bean instances.

🧠 Why @Scope is used
--> Control bean instance creation
--> Define whether a bean is shared or new per request

Common Bean Scopes : 
| Scope                 | Meaning                           |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `singleton` (default) | One instance per Spring container |
| `prototype`           | New instance every time           |
| `request`             | One per HTTP request              |
| `session`             | One per HTTP session              |
| `application`         | One per web application           |
| `websocket`           | One per WebSocket                 |

Ex: Singleton (Default)
@Component
@Scope("singleton")
public class UserService {
}
--> ✔ Same object reused

Ex: Prototype
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class TokenGenerator {
}
--> ✔ New object every injection

Ex: Web Scope
@Component
@Scope(value = WebApplicationContext.SCOPE_REQUEST)
public class RequestData {
}

⚠️ Important Interview Points
--> Singleton beans live from startup to shutdown

--> Prototype beans:
           1. Spring creates
           2. Spring does NOT destroy
           3. @PreDestroy ❌ for prototype

15. @Profile :
--> It tells Spring to load a bean or configuration only when a specific environment profile is active.

🧠 Why @Profile is used
--> Environment-specific configuration
--> Separate dev / test / prod behavior
--> Avoids manual code changes between environments

Ex: 
application.yml
----------------
spring:
  profiles:
    active: dev

Bean Definitions
------------------
1.DevDataSourceConfig
@Component
@Profile("dev")
public class DevDataSourceConfig {
}

2.ProdDataSourceConfig
@Component
@Profile("prod")
public class ProdDataSourceConfig {
}
Note: Only DevDataSourceConfig loads when dev is active

🔄 How it works (Simple) : 
      Active profile checked
             ↓
      Matching beans loaded

Q: Where You Can Use @Profile
--> On @Component
--> On @Configuration
--> On @Bean methods

