2️⃣ Spring MVC / REST Annotations
----------------------------------------
| Annotation              | Purpose / Short Explanation                                  |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `@RestController`       | Combines `@Controller` + `@ResponseBody`, used for REST APIs |
| `@Controller`           | Handles HTTP requests; returns view                          |
| `@RequestMapping`       | Maps HTTP requests (GET, POST, etc.)                         |
| `@GetMapping`           | Shortcut for GET request                                     |
| `@PostMapping`          | Shortcut for POST request                                    |
| `@PutMapping`           | Shortcut for PUT request                                     |
| `@DeleteMapping`        | Shortcut for DELETE request                                  |
| `@PathVariable`         | Binds URI template variable to method parameter              |
| `@RequestParam`         | Binds query parameter to method parameter                    |
| `@RequestBody`          | Maps HTTP request body (JSON → Object)                       |
| `@ResponseBody`         | Converts return object to JSON response                      |
| `@CrossOrigin`          | Enables CORS for REST API                                    |
| `@ExceptionHandler`     | Handles exceptions in controller methods                     |
| `@RestControllerAdvice` | Global exception handler for REST APIs                       |
| `@ModelAttribute`       | Binds request parameters to method/model object              |
| `@Valid`                | Triggers validation for DTOs                                 |

1. @RestController
-->  @RestController is used to build REST APIs and automatically converts Java objects into JSON responses.”
-->  It combines @Controller + @ResponseBody, meaning all methods return data directly as JSON/XML, not views.
Ex: 
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping
    public List<User> getAllUsers() {
        return userService.getAllUsers();
    }

    @PostMapping
    public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
        return userService.createUser(user);
    }
}

2. @Controller : 
--> @Controller is a Spring MVC annotation used to handle web requests and return views (JSP, Thymeleaf, HTML).
--> It is mainly used in traditional MVC applications, not pure REST APIs.
Ex: 
@Controller
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping("/page")
    public String userPage(Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("users", userService.getAllUsers());
        return "users"; // returns users.html
    }
}

Q: @Controller vs @RestController

| Feature                    | `@Controller`                                                   | `@RestController`                                                 |
| -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**                | Handles web requests and returns **views** (HTML/JSP/Thymeleaf) | Handles web requests and returns **JSON/XML** directly (REST API) |
| **Response Type**          | View name (requires a template engine like Thymeleaf)           | JSON/XML (no view rendering)                                      |
| **Annotation Composition** | Standalone                                                      | Combination of `@Controller + @ResponseBody`                      |
| **Typical Use Case**       | Traditional Spring MVC web applications                         | RESTful web services / APIs                                       |
| **Method Return**          | Returns **String** (view name) or `ModelAndView`                | Returns **Java objects**, automatically serialized as JSON/XML    |
| **Default Serialization**  | ❌ Not serialized (needs `@ResponseBody` for JSON)              | ✅ Automatically serialized to JSON/XML                          |


3. @RequestMapping : 
--> “@RequestMapping is a generic mapping annotation used to define base URLs and map multiple HTTP methods, while specific annotations like @GetMapping provide better clarity.”

4. @GetMapping : 
--> @GetMapping is used to handle HTTP GET requests and it is used to retrieve (read-only) data from the database through an API.

Q: @GetMapping does directly access the database ?
--> @GetMapping does not directly access the database
--> It calls the service layer, which then interacts with the repository (JPA) to fetch data .

5. @PostMapping : 
--> @PostMapping is used to handle HTTP POST requests and it is used to create/save new data into the database through a REST API.

6. @PutMapping : 
-->  @PutMapping is used to handle HTTP PUT requests and it is used to update an existing resource completely in the database through a REST API.

7. @PatchMapping
-->  @PatchMapping is used to handle HTTP PATCH requests and it is used to partially update an existing resource in the database.

8. DeleteMapping : 
--> @DeleteMapping is used to handle HTTP DELETE requests and it is used to remove data from the database through a REST API.

9. @PathVariable : 
--> @PathVariable is used to extract values from URI path segments in a REST API.
-->  It allows you to capture dynamic values from the URL and use them as method parameters.
Ex: 
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public User getUserById(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return userService.getUserById(id);
    }
}
--> URL: GET /users/5
--> @PathVariable captures 5 and passes it as id to the method.

Q. URI Vs URL ?
--> URI (Uniform Resource Identifier): Identifies a resource on the server (can be just a path like /users/5).
--> URL (Uniform Resource Locator): Is a type of URI that also includes protocol, domain, and port (like http://localhost:8080/users/5).

10. @RequestParam : 
--> @RequestParam is used to extract query parameters from the URL(after ?) in a REST API.
--> It only captures query parameter values and passes them to the controller method, which then calls the service layer and the repository interacts with the database.

Ex: 
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping
    public List<User> getUsersByRole(@RequestParam String role) {
        return userService.getUsersByRole(role);
    }
}
--> URL: GET /users?role=admin
--> @RequestParam captures role=admin and passes it to the method.

Q: @RequestParam Vs @PathVariable ?
--> “@RequestParam is used to extract query parameters from the URL, while @PathVariable extracts values from URI path segments.”

11. @RequestBody : 
--> @RequestBody is used to converts the incoming request body into a Java object.
--> It is mainly used with POST or PUT requests to receive JSON (or XML) data from the client.
Ex: 
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @PostMapping
    public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
        return userService.createUser(user);
    }
}
HTTP POST Body (JSON):
{
  "name": "Ajay",
  "email": "ajay@gmail.com",
  "phone": "7542055096"
}
--> @RequestBody converts this JSON into a User object automatically.

12. @ResponseBody : 
--> “@ResponseBody converts Java objects (returned by controller methods) into JSON/XML responses instead of rendering a view.”
Ex: 
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    @ResponseBody
    public User getUserById(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return userService.getUserById(id);
    }
}
--> Here, @ResponseBody ensures the User object is converted to JSON and sent in the HTTP response.

13. @CrossOrigin: 
--> @CrossOrigin is used to enable CORS and allow frontend applications hosted on different origins to access backend APIs.
--> It helps to resolve CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) issues when a frontend and backend run on different origins.
Ex: 
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
@CrossOrigin(origins = "http://localhost:3000")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping
    public List<User> getAllUsers() {
        return userService.getAllUsers();
    }
}
--> Allows requests from React app running on port 3000.

14. @ExceptionHandler : 
--> @ExceptionHandler is used to handle specific exceptions thrown from controller methods and
    return a custom response instead of a default error. 
OR,
--> “@ExceptionHandler is used to handle controller-level exceptions and return meaningful HTTP responses.”
Ex: 
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public User getUser(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return userService.getUserById(id);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                             .body(ex.getMessage());
    }
}
--> If ResourceNotFoundException occurs → custom 404 response is returned.

15. @RestControllerAdvice : 
--> @RestControllerAdvice is used to create a global exception handler for all REST controllers.
--> It combines @ControllerAdvice + @ResponseBody, so all exception responses are returned as JSON/XML.
Ex: 
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                             .body(ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<String> handleGeneric(Exception ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
                             .body("Something went wrong");
    }
}

16. @ModelAttribute : 
--> @ModelAttribute binds form data or request parameters to Java objects and is mainly used in Spring MVC applications.”
Ex: 
@Controller
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @PostMapping("/save")
    public String saveUser(@ModelAttribute User user) {
        userService.saveUser(user);
        return "success";
    }
}
--> HTML Form → automatically mapped to User object
Key Points : 
--> Used mostly with @Controller, not REST APIs
--> Works with form submissions
--> Automatically adds the object to the model

17. @Valid : 
--> @Valid is used to trigger validation on a request object using Bean Validation (JSR-380) annotations such as @NotNull, @Size, @Email, etc.
Ex: 
@PostMapping("/users")
public ResponseEntity<String> createUser(@Valid @RequestBody UserDTO userDTO) {
    userService.createUser(userDTO);
    return ResponseEntity.ok("User created");
}

✅ DTO with Validation Annotations
public class UserDTO {

    @NotBlank
    private String name;

    @Email
    @NotBlank
    private String email;

    @Size(min = 10, max = 10)
    private String phone;
}
--> If validation fails → MethodArgumentNotValidException is thrown

Key Points: 
--> Works with @RequestBody, @ModelAttribute, @PathVariable
--> Prevents invalid data from entering business logic
--> Requires spring-boot-starter-validation
